+\subsection{Symbolic representations and rewrite search}
+Reduced ordered binary decision diagrams provide a canonical function
+representation for a fixed variable order \citep{bryant1986}; their graph
+size and apply-operation cost must be measured under that order. Boolean
+polynomial systems such as PolyBoRi instead exploit zero-suppressed decision
+diagrams and shared recursive arithmetic \citep{brickenstein2008}. An expanded
+list-of-monomials ANF baseline therefore does not stand in for every symbolic
+polynomial method. For an ANF-output task, a fair comparison charges conversion
+and any required monomial enumeration; for a function-evaluation task, it
+should preserve the comparator's compact representation where permitted.
+
+Equality saturation retains equivalent expressions and extracts a result
+under a chosen cost model \citep{willsey2021}. Synthesis-based superoptimizers
+such as Souper search for equivalent replacements in a compiler IR
+\citep{sasnauskas2017}. These are broader searches than the present fixed
+two-operand rule. Giving both methods the same local truth-function operation
+is necessary before interpreting a difference as CM-specific. Recognition,
+search, extraction and conversion costs then belong to the measured endpoint.
+Similarly, partial evaluation under fixed inputs and memoization of shared
+nodes are engineering baselines, not effects uniquely supplied by CM notation.
+
 
